  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nPrimary boundary stretches from just north of Quincy to near La\nSalle this hour, while satellite continues to show an area of\nACCAS just south of it. Temperatures south of the boundary are\nwidely in the mid 90s, with a few mesonet sites south of I-72\neven warmer. The cap has eroded and mesoanalysis indicates about\n2500 J/kg of SBCAPE near this boundary, along with about 30\nknots of 0-6km shear. So far the convection has remained fairly\nscattered and mostly thunder free, though an uptick in \nconvection is expected toward mid afternoon where cumulus clouds\nhave recently been blossoming. A couple main periods of storms\nare expected, one through about sunset, then an uptick in\ndevelopment from the west mid to late evening and past midnight. \nBesides the threat of some isolated damaging wind gusts, high\nprecipitable water values over 2 inches (near the top of\nclimatology for mid September) will produce areas of heavy rain.\nLPMM guidance from the HREF suggests potential for 2-3 inches in\nswaths of heavy rain, especially this evening when more in the\nway of cell training is expected. Flash flood guidance is 2-2.5\ninches in an hour across much of central Illinois, so there\ncould be some localized flooding if the rain comes down too\nfast. \n\nBy Wednesday morning, the front is expected to be somewhere\nbetween I-72 and I-70, where it should loiter for a day or so as\nit becomes parallel to the upper flow. Rain chances will\ncontinue into Thursday north of the boundary, with the heavy\nrain threat gradually shifting into northern Illinois. By late\nweek, the front should be back north of the area, before a\nshortwave arrives to start the weekend. The boundary is expected\nto stall once again over central Illinois. As an upper low\ncloses off over the northern Plains and upper Mississippi Valley\nearly next week, any push of this front out of the area will be\nslow. Thus, rain chances will linger over portions of central\nIllinois, though they will be highest Saturday night into Sunday\nevening.\n\nTemperature-wise, a significant range in highs is expected over\nthe forecast area through the weekend. Highs in the 90s will\ncontinue south of the boundary. North of there, 80s will be more\ncommon, though with lower clouds and rain on Wednesday, areas\nnorth of Havana and Bloomington are likely to remain in the 70s.",
